**Stub.** This page gathers the Concepts section of each milestone. Each milestone page explains its concepts with citations. Single terms live in the [glossary](/glossary).

## M1. Bench it

The robot system and its 10 ms packet cycle, steppers and drivers at 32 microsteps, why the E-stop is normally closed, and why the mains switch is the stop that still works. See [M1 concepts](/lore/m1-bench-it#concepts).

## M2. One joint end to end

Planetary gearbox ratio and step math, backlash, how an inductive sensor sees a steel screw head, couplers and threadlocker, and why the parts are printed in PETG. See [M2 concepts](/lore/m2-one-joint#concepts).

## M3. The whole arm moves

Joint numbering, limits and directions, kinematics, homing as the only position reference, and how an open-loop stepper loses steps. See [M3 concepts](/lore/m3-whole-arm#concepts).

## M4. Pick and place

The tool frame and the TCP, joint moves and Cartesian moves, what a repeatability of 0.1 mm means, and the 10 ms packet cycle. See [M4 concepts](/lore/m4-pick-and-place#concepts).

## M5. Touch the skiff

**Our design.** Coordinate frames and registration, Eurorack mechanics, and why compliance matters. See [M5 concepts](/lore/m5-touch-the-skiff#concepts).

## M6. Patch a cable

**Our design.** Peg-in-hole insertion, spiral-search and wiggle strategies, and detection without a force sensor. See [M6 concepts](/lore/m6-patch-a-cable#concepts).

## M7. Play it

**Our design.** Sequencing against a musical clock, MIDI and CV control, and handling failure mid-performance. See [M7 concepts](/lore/m7-play-it#concepts).

## Terms

- **mental model**: the why behind a step. Every milestone page gives its concepts their own section, apart from the how-to steps, following [four-component instructional design](https://www.4cid.org/).
